## Chapter 1.3: The Charred Body

Luka pieced together the fragmented headlines and morning radio bulletins lingering in his memory, trying to recall the person in the picture. Finally, he got an answer.

“Igor Romashin… Is he the director of logistics from Khabarovsk who was accused of embezzlement a few days ago?”

“Yup, exactly,” Nadezhda nodded then chuckled. “Heh, I hope you didn’t just use your vision just now. You do know quite a lot, Comrade Huo, maybe more than you should,” she teased, lightly drumming her fingers against the wooden counter.

Luka returned a smile before saying, “Well, to answer your question: No, I haven’t seen him.”

She nodded knowingly, already half-expecting the answer. While she could bid farewell right now, she hesitated. Internally, her professionalism and her instinct bickered. What she wanted to say was confidential, but there was something about Luka that she found _special_. Perhaps his intellectual authority and quiet earnestness made him more trustworthy in her eyes—or perhaps she was simply dying to share the burden of a secret.

“In that case, you’ve missed your chance…” She led him on, knowing that his curiosity would want to know more.

“What do you mean?” He replied, as expected.

She leaned in close over the glass partition, whispering, “If I tell you this, you have to keep it a secret, okay?”

“Uh… are you sure about this? If this is confidential, I’d rather—”

“Yes, it’s confidential and it’s unprofessional, but sooner or later you will hear about it anyway.”

Luka wanted to refuse, but his curiosity wouldn’t let him. What could have been so important that this officer wanted to break protocol to tell him?

“…Fine. Let’s hear it.”

Nadezhda took a moment to paraphrase the news, glancing left and right toward the empty hallway. “This morning, Igor Romashin was found dead in his sports car, burned to a crisp. Right here in Pogranichny.”

Luka was taken aback. A high-profile fugitive ended up dead in this town? Burned to a crisp, no less. In a peaceful socialist era where violent crime was an absolute statistical anomaly, homicide was something people rarely ever had to think about.

“…I— Wow. I mean, when did this happen?” Luka asked the only question that came to his mind.

“We don’t know for sure, but a citizen reported a fire around 4:30. I wasn’t on duty at the time,” which was a formal way of saying that she was fast asleep under her warm blanket, “the first responders along with firefighters arrived at the scene, put out the fire, and discovered the body.”

“But that’s all I heard,” she looked to the side with disappointment in her eyes, slumping back in her swivel chair. “It sucks missing out on all the action and sitting quietly here.” At this point, she dropped all formality and spoke to Luka like a friend, “Imagine it, even a patrol officer might have gotten a sneak peek. Ugh, this is killing me, comrade. Out of all the times it could have happened, it just has to be on my shift…” She let out a big, dramatic sigh that ruffled the bangs on her forehead.

Luka was caught in the awkwardness; he couldn’t just get up and leave. She was venting to him not as an officer to a citizen, but as a comrade to a comrade. He didn’t quite know how to comment since he had no clue how the Militsiya worked, but as an academic who frequently navigated lab schedules, talking about shifts, he might know a thing or two.

“Um… could someone cover your shift for you?” Luka quietly suggested.

“Covering my shift, huh…” Nadezhda pondered. That thought hadn’t crossed her mind because she didn’t want to be penalized this early in her career. But hearing it out loud, she realized it might just work.

“Yeah. Yeah. I think that’s a great idea, Comrade Huo!” 

She quickly turned to her terminal, tapping fast on the clattering keyboard. Her smirk widened every second, like a mastermind seeing their plan fit together. Luka could only sit there on the bench, wondering if he had just accidentally enabled a junior officer to go rogue.

A moment later, the magnetic coupler chimed. She snatched a freshly synced sheet of e-paper rolled it neatly in her hand. “Now, comrade, we just have to wait for the final piece…” She smiled mischievously.

Luka raised his eyebrow in confusion. Nadezhda was definitely more of a wild card than he’d imagined. As he was about to ask what she meant, she cut him off.

“And… here comes the devil,” she leaned to the right to look past Luka toward the entrance. 

Luka looked behind him and saw a senior officer stepping through the heavy storm doors. As he turned back, Nadezhda said to him, “Okay, wait here a moment,” then stood up and walked gleefully right over to greet the older officer.

“Good morning, Uncle Senya!” She saluted with a bright smile.

A silver-haired senior officer, probably in his late 40s to early 50s, stomped the Siberian snow from his heavy felt boots and slowly took off his thick winter coat, revealing his uniform underneath. The insignia on his shoulder boards bore the broad, golden-yellow longitudinal stripe of a veteran Starshina.

“To you too, Nadya,” he saluted back in a gravelly, grandfatherly voice. He knew that attitude all too well—the hyper-activeness, the unnaturally friendly smile. It was clear that she wanted something. “So… How can I help you, lass?”

“Have you been to Igor’s crime scene, Uncle?” She asked.

Senya gazed upward, scratching his chin through his ushanka as he tried to remember, “Igor? Crime scene?” It seemed he was oblivious to the recent events.

“Oh. I thought you knew,” she tried to explain concisely, keeping her voice low. “Well, Igor is a high-profile fugitive from Khabarovsk. We found him dead this morning, burned to a crisp―”

“Woah, woah, stop right there! Don’t fill my head with gory images! It will ruin my morning tea time,” Senya stopped her story. He wasn’t very fond of death.

“Ah, sorry, Uncle,” she tilted her head slightly, “but let me get to the point. Can you cover for me for an hour, Uncle?” She gave him the pleading eyes of the youth.

“Eh… but my shift starts at noon. What’s the occasion?” Senya hesitated, not wanting to agree just out of whim.

“I haven’t seen a real crime scene before, Uncle. Please… I’ll buy something on the way back for you. Snacks, teas, fidget toys, whatever you want!” Nadezhda went full throttle on her plea, clasping her hands together.

Senya let out a hearty chuckle. “Alright, alright. A senior like me should support an aspiring youth after all. Just don’t take too long now,” he approved. “And also… get me an oolong tea,” he smirked.

“Thank you, Uncle Senya!” She saluted him again and trotted back to Luka.

Luka overheard the entire conversation. It seemed he shouldn’t underestimate how scheming she was; given the chance, she would bend every protocol if it got her what she set her mind to.

“That went better than expected,” she said, standing there proud of what she had achieved. “Now, Comrade Huo, want to tag along? Provided that you are free from your academic duties,” she invited.

Luka thought, “_Oh great, now she’s just going to keep dragging me around like her partner-in-crime,_” but a seed of curiosity was already planted inside him. Besides, what else was he going to do for the rest of the day anyway? He had already called in sick at PIRM, his wormhole calculation was already done, and it wasn't like he was in the mood to go drink with Alex.

“…Well, since I already called in sick at PIRM…” He tried to sound reluctant, not wanting to look like a total pushover.

Her eyes lit up. Since Luka arrived, her day just kept getting better and better. “Perfect! Let me grab my winter coat.” She scuttled around the desk to fetch her gear.

Senya, who had just walked behind his side of the desk, cradling a steaming glass mug of tea, glanced briefly at Luka, then turned to Nadezhda and teased, “Well, well, Nadya, since when did you get a boyfriend?”

Nadezhda froze mid-button, rolled her eyes lightly, and let out a small chuckle. “Ugh, Uncle, I’ve already told you that I like women, haven’t I?”

“I don’t know, maybe you realized that you’re bisexual, haha,” he laughed. Nadezhda shook her head with an exasperated groan to dismiss his silly hypothesis.

During that time, Luka buttoned his winter jacket and pulled his wool scarf tight, getting ready to head out into the cold Siberian winter again.

“All set, comrade. Follow me!” She walked ahead to the door, leading the way.

“Good luck out there!” Senya shouted from behind the desk. Luka and Nadezhda looked back and nodded before pushing through the double storm doors into the biting April wind.
